*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    REMOTE.API ON | LOCAL | OFF {RESTART | NO.RESTART}
*
*    RESTART or NO.RESTART answers in advance the question this verb asks when the listener moves (on, local and off are three
*    different listeners): restart SD now (every session ends) or leave the change for SD's next start.  Without either word the
*    question is asked.
*
*    Says whether this machine provides the SD API, and who may reach it.
*
*       ON     SD listens on port 4247 on every address and other computers may connect
*       LOCAL  SD listens on port 4247 at 127.0.0.1 only (APILOCAL=1) and only this computer may connect
*       OFF    SD opens no API socket at all
*
*    15 Nov 26 (RELEASE_1.1 317): LOCAL was every address with the firewall shut; it binds the loopback address now, as SD Core
*    for Linux's LOCAL, so a move between ON and LOCAL moves the listener too and is offered the restart.
*
*    With no keyword it reports the current setting and changes nothing.
*
* ***THERE ARE TWO AXES AND THIS VERB SETS BOTH.***  APIPORT in sd.conf decides
* whether SD opens a socket; the firewall rule decides who may reach it.  LOCAL
* exists because they are separate - listener up, firewall shut - and it is the
* state PRE_RELEASE 75 removed from the installer when the API box became a
* service switch.  The owner ruled it back in here on 30 Aug 2026, which is why
* it is three keywords and not the two he first named.
*
* ***THE LISTENER ONLY OPENS AT START-UP, SO ON AND OFF NEED A RESTART.***
* gplbld/stage.py:499 records it: open_api_listener() returns -1 for "no
* listener" when the port is <= 0, read once as SD starts.  The restart is asked
* for ONLY when the listener moved - api-listener.ps1 reports "already ON",
* "already LOCAL" or "already OFF" and this reads that.  (Until 15 Nov 26 LOCAL
* and ON differed only in the firewall; RELEASE_1.1 317 made LOCAL a loopback bind.)
*
* ***THE RESTART DISCONNECTS THE CALLER, AND THAT IS WHY THE WARNING COMES
* FIRST.***  Measured in the C on 30 Aug 2026: stop_sd() (gplsrc/sysseg.c:766)
* walks the user table and SIGTERMs every entry with a uid and a pid > 0.  It
* has no "are users logged in" check - cycle.ps1's comment saying it refuses is
* wrong.  So the administrator running this verb is one of the sessions it
* ends, and they will not be here to read a message printed afterwards.  The
* Y/N question therefore states the consequence, and nothing is printed after
* the restart that matters.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal

program $remote.api
$catalog $REMOTEAPI

* THE SAME FOUR MODIFY.ACCOUNT AND DELETE.ACCOUNT TAKE, and int$keys.h is not
* optional here: K$ADMINISTRATOR (:101) and K$WINPATH (:140) are BOTH defined
* there and in neither of the others.  Leaving it out compiles to an
* unknown-symbol failure that costs a whole cycle to discover.
$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h

   deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

* 31 Aug 26 Windows port - the reporting form, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90.  Only
*   report.state uses it; the listener, firewall and restart calls keep
*   ps_script, because each already has a message of its own for what it did.
   deffun ps_script_out(script, want.out) calling '!ps_script_out'

   prompt ''

*  ADMINISTRATOR ONLY, THE SAME GATE MODIFY.ACCOUNT USES (MODIFYA:181).  This
*  changes what the machine offers the network; it is not an ordinary user's.
   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

*  ASSIGNED HERE FOR BCOMP, NOT FOR THE LOGIC.  bootstrap.py fails the WHOLE
*  bootstrap on an "is not assigned a value" warning, and BCOMP reads the
*  source in order.  Every value is also the safe one: no action, no change.
   action     = ''
   want.port  = ''
   want.fw    = ''
   port.moved = @false
   rc         = 0
   yn         = ''
   report     = ''
   both       = ''
   r.i        = 0
   rpt        = ''
   rpt.cp     = 0

   parser = "!PARSER"
   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)   ;* Verb remote.api
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)  ;* ON | LOCAL | OFF

   action = upcase(trim(action))

*  NO KEYWORD IS A REPORT, NOT AN ERROR.  Asking what the setting is should
*  never be able to change it, so this path runs api-listener.ps1 -Show and
*  stops before anything else.
   if token.type = PARSER$END or action = '' then
      display sysmsg(12950)   ;* Reading the setting (a few seconds)...  (owner, 12 Nov 26: a line before any wait over 2 s; RELEASE_1.1 243)
      gosub report.state
      stop
   end

   begin case
      case action = 'ON'
         want.port = '-On'
         want.fw   = '-Open'
      case action = 'LOCAL'
         want.port = '-Local'     ;* 15 Nov 26 (RELEASE_1.1 317): APILOCAL=1, the listener binds 127.0.0.1 only, as Linux's LOCAL
         want.fw   = '-Restrict'
      case action = 'OFF'
         want.port = '-Off'
         want.fw   = '-Restrict'
      case @true
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         stop sysmsg(10131)  ;* remote.api on | local | off
   end case

*  15 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 314 (owner: "put the restart question in the page's dialog").  RESTART or NO.RESTART after the word answers
*  THE RESTART question below in advance, so tool.remote can ask it in its own dialog and run this verb with no terminal.  Neither word: the question
*  is asked here, as before.  The restart still happens only when the listener moved.  Read BEFORE anything is changed, so a wrong word changes nothing.
   want.restart = ''
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
   if token.type # PARSER$END then
      begin case
         case upcase(token) = 'RESTART'
            want.restart = 'Y'
         case upcase(token) = 'NO.RESTART'
            want.restart = 'N'
         case @true
            @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
            stop sysmsg(10131)  ;* remote.api on | local | off {restart | no.restart}
      end case
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
      if token.type # PARSER$END then
         @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
         stop sysmsg(10131)
      end
   end

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  1.  THE LISTENER.  api-listener.ps1 edits APIPORT in sd.conf and reads the
*      record back before reporting; see its own header for why it is a script
*      rather than BASIC in here.
*
*      EXIT 0 MEANS "the file now says what was asked", which includes the case
*      where it already did - so a second "remote.api on" is not an error.  It
*      prints "already ON" and this verb learns from the SECOND script, below,
*      whether anything actually needs restarting.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   display sysmsg(12944)   ;* Making the change (a few seconds)...  (RELEASE_1.1 243: two PowerShell scripts follow)
   gosub run.listener
   if rc # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(10133, downcase(action), rc)  ;* Could not change the API listener
   end

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  2.  THE FIREWALL.  api-firewall.ps1 is the installer's own script and is
*      already shipped and re-runnable; -Open and -Restrict are its two states.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   gosub run.firewall
   if rc # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(10134, action, rc)  ;* The listener was set but the firewall was not
   end

*  6 Oct 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 128.  BOTH HALVES TOOK EFFECT, so the change
*  is audited, in the words SD Core for Linux uses.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'remote.api ' : downcase(action))   ;* on, local or off, lower case (owner's rule 7 Oct; Linux T1730)
   @system.return.code = 0

   crt sysmsg(10132, action)  ;* The SD API is now %1

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  3.  THE RESTART, AND ONLY IF THE LISTENER ACTUALLY MOVED.
*
*      A MOVE OF THE LISTENER IS WHAT NEEDS IT: off, on (every address) and local
*      (127.0.0.1, since 15 Nov 26, RELEASE_1.1 317) are three listeners.  port.moved
*      is set by run.listener from what the script reported rather than from what
*      was asked for, so "remote.api on" twice asks once.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   if not(port.moved) then
      stop
   end

*  ***THE PROMPT SAYS WHICH ANSWER ENTER GIVES, AND ENTER GIVES IT.***  Owner's
*  rule, 30 Aug 2026: "scripts need to indicate which is the default using
*  <y>/n or y/<n>".  The default here is N, and it is the safe one - it changes
*  nothing that has not already been written and disconnects nobody.  The two
*  halves have to agree: a prompt that shows a default and then loops on an
*  empty answer is worse than one that shows none.
   if want.restart # '' then
      yn = want.restart        ;* 15 Nov 26 (RELEASE_1.1 314): answered in advance by RESTART or NO.RESTART
   end else
   loop
      display sysmsg(10135) :   ;* Restart SD now? Every session ends (y/<n>)?
      input yn
      yn = upcase(trim(yn))
      if yn = '' then yn = 'N'
      until yn = 'Y' or yn = 'N'
   repeat
   end

   if yn = 'N' then
      stop sysmsg(10136)  ;* Not restarted - it takes effect when SD is next started
   end

*  NOTHING AFTER THIS LINE IS SEEN BY THE CALLER, and that is by design rather
*  than by accident - this session is SIGTERMed with the rest.  The Y/N above is
*  the last thing they read.  18 Nov 26 (RELEASE_1.1 340): the restart is handed
*  to Task Scheduler (restart-sd.ps1 -Detach, a one-shot SYSTEM task), because
*  the elevated helper is tied to this session and died with it, leaving SD
*  stopped and never started (measured 18 Nov 01:25).
   gosub run.restart
   stop

* ===========================================================================

*  ***THIS PRINTED NOTHING UNTIL 31 Aug 2026.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90.***  The
*  description above promises "with no keyword it reports the current setting",
*  and ps_script returns a status while the elevated helper threw the script's
*  output away.  This verb is administrator-gated, so it ALWAYS took that
*  branch - the unelevated fall-through that would have reached the terminal is
*  unreachable here.  Measured on the installed tree: "remote.api" in SDSYS
*  printed nothing at all.
*
*  Field 1 is the status ps_script would have returned; fields 2 onwards are
*  what the script printed.
report.state:
*  THE LISTENER: whether SD opens a socket at all.
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-listener.ps1') : char(39) : ' -Show'
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   both   = ps_script_out(ps, @true)
   rc     = field(both, @fm, 1)
   report = field(both, @fm, 2, 9999)
   gosub print.report
   if rc # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10133, 'show', rc)  ;* Could not read the API listener setting
   end

*  03 Sep 26 - AND WHO MAY REACH IT, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 148.  The listener above
*  says whether a socket is open; this says who may reach it.  With no keyword
*  the verb reported only the first, so on a machine that kept its database
*  through a reinstall (147) it answered "listening" while nothing could
*  connect.  api-firewall.ps1 -Show now ends on a plain sentence, the same shape
*  REMOTESSH prints from ssh-firewall.ps1, and this reads it the same way.
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-firewall.ps1') : char(39) : ' -Show'
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   both   = ps_script_out(ps, @true)
   rc     = field(both, @fm, 1)
   report = field(both, @fm, 2, 9999)
   gosub print.report
   if rc # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10133, 'show', rc)  ;* Could not read the API firewall setting
   end
   return

*  THE STRIP-AND-PRINT LOOP, SHARED BY THE TWO READS ABOVE.  Field by field,
*  with the script's "name:" prefix stripped - PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 115: each line
*  is tagged with its filename, and a leading "token: " (colon-space, no space
*  before it) is removed so the report does not lead with the script's name.  A
*  genuine "x : y" line is left alone.  (crt of a dynamic array would print the
*  field marks themselves, hence field by field.)
print.report:
   if report # '' then
      for r.i = 1 to dcount(report, @fm)
         rpt    = report<r.i>
         rpt.cp = index(rpt, ': ', 1)
         if rpt.cp and index(rpt[1, rpt.cp], ' ', 1) = 0 then
            rpt = rpt[rpt.cp + 2, len(rpt)]
         end
         crt rpt
      next r.i
   end
   return

run.listener:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-listener.ps1') : char(39) : ' ' : want.port : ' -ReportChange'
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)

*  13 Nov 26 - RELEASE_1.1 278.  "ALREADY" IS VISIBLE THROUGH THE EXIT CODE NOW.
*  With -ReportChange the script exits 0 when it moved the listener and 3 when
*  it was already in the wanted state (a success, nothing to restart for).  The
*  rule this replaced offered the restart only for the WORDS on and off, so
*  LOCAL from OFF - which turns the listener on - was never offered it, leaving
*  "a setting that does not take" until SD's next start, the case its own
*  comment said must not happen; and ON twice asked twice.
   port.moved = (rc = 0)
   if rc = 3 then rc = 0
   return

run.firewall:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-firewall.ps1') : char(39) : ' ' : want.fw
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)
   return

run.restart:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/restart-sd.ps1') : char(39) : ' -Detach'   ;* 18 Nov 26: RELEASE_1.1 340
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)
   return

end

* END-CODE
